WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Speaker 1: There's been a dramatic turn of events&nbsp;
in 2022, polling over the last couple of months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.640
And I don't want to overstate this, but I also&nbsp;
don't want to ignore the story because when three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.960
four months ago, I was saying, guys, listen,&nbsp;
everyone must vote and we have to do everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.760
fight for every seat, all of these things. But&nbsp;
it's not looking great for Democrats in 2022. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.880
think Democrats will lose the House. I think&nbsp;
Democrats will lose the Senate. Some of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.360
wrote to me and said, David, why are you writing&nbsp;
it off? You should just say we can win it. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.200
I'm not writing it off. I'm just telling you&nbsp;
what the polling is and I'm being realistic. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.040
polling has reversed, reversed dramatically.&nbsp;
And Democrats are now ahead of Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.800
in three recent polls looking at a generic 2022&nbsp;
ballot. A generic 2022 ballot means regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.400
where you are, you plan to vote for the Democrat&nbsp;
or for the Republican. This is not normal. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.240
again, now there's the risk of another unintended&nbsp;
consequence of mentioning this, which is if I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.360
oh, Democrats are doing great now, then people&nbsp;
will say, Well, I don't need to vote. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.880
quite frankly think this show is big enough to&nbsp;
have that much of an effect one way or the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:11.920
But we all must vote. But we should understand the&nbsp;
numbers. Three new polls in a new Marist poll in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:19.520
congressional ballot. Democrats, 48, Republicans,&nbsp;
41. That's a plus seven. For Democrats, that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:28.320
big, big deal. In a new Politico morning consult&nbsp;
poll, Democrats 45. Republicans 42. That's plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:36.960
three for Democrats. And then in a new Yahoo&nbsp;
News YouGov poll, Democrats, 45. Republicans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:44.080
38. That's plus seven for Democrats. Why are these&nbsp;
numbers such a big deal? There's two reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:50.000
Number one, this is not typically what you see&nbsp;
when one party has taken the White House in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.040
previous election, in 2020, Democrats took the&nbsp;
White House from Republicans. Biden beat Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:02.400
You all know that. And typically, almost always&nbsp;
with like three exceptions. When that happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.640
you would expect Republicans to do really well in&nbsp;
the first midterms, which would be four and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.440
months from now in November. So, number one, the&nbsp;
fact that the numbers differ from that traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.880
historical reality is a really big deal. But the&nbsp;
second reason that it's a really big deal is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.560
is not the way the numbers look just three months&nbsp;
ago. And I think that we can all sort of take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.840
cynical approach here and say this is temporary.&nbsp;
It'll go back to Republicans crushing Democrats by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.480
November. Well, it's not obvious to me, but one of&nbsp;
the things we've been talking about now for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:41.920
two months is, are there unique circumstances in&nbsp;
this election that make it potentially okay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.080
Democrats when Republicans should be winning?&nbsp;
And I say that the answer is yes. This is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.160
guarantee of anything. I'm still getting a lot&nbsp;
of people on Twitter saying, David, you loser.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.880
Some of them say worse things than that.&nbsp;
Republicans are going to crush Democrats in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.240
November. Just wait and see. Well, maybe, but&nbsp;
there are some different circumstances here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:08.160
First of all, normally when we say, oh, Democrats&nbsp;
can win by running on abortion rights, know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.800
historically that's not a good issue for Democrats&nbsp;
to run on. It's not a winning issue. It's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.400
voting issue for Democrats. It's the type of issue&nbsp;
that will motivate Republicans to come out to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.680
against a pro-choice candidate. Typically, that's&nbsp;
the way it is this time around, it looks like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.600
could be different. Americans are more in favor&nbsp;
of abortion being legal in most cases than at any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.520
time over the last 50 years. So public opinion is&nbsp;
different now than it has been on abortion. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:43.760
addition to that, Republican appointed Republican&nbsp;
nominee Trump nominated Supreme Court justices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.800
have overturn Roe v Wade, something which two&nbsp;
thirds of Americans are in disagreement with. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.560
is different. And that means in this election,&nbsp;
that situation could make it better for Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.280
particularly if they say we're the ones trying to&nbsp;
keep these rights, whether it's credible or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.080
that that's what Democrats are going to do.&nbsp;
Secondly, normally running on gun safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.600
regulation is a losing issue for Democrats.&nbsp;
We've talked about it before. We've talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.880
about it with a number of political strategists.&nbsp;
When Democrats make gun safety regulations a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.760
part of their campaign, it doesn't really&nbsp;
motivate Democrats to come out and vote for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.880
but it does motivate Republicans to come out and&nbsp;
vote against them. However, the rash of school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.000
and other mass shootings in the United States over&nbsp;
the last three months and just tragic numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.480
hundreds of mass shootings in the United States in&nbsp;
2022 so far seem to have tired out and exhausted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.720
many Americans about the Republican Party's&nbsp;
unwillingness to consider serious gun safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.320
regulations. And so you have a situation where&nbsp;
in this particular midterm, it might actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.880
work for Democrats to run on gun safety and&nbsp;
on abortion rights. So that's. The normal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.720
The other element that is different than normal&nbsp;
in these midterms is that we are coming up against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.640
the tail end of this six year Trumpian cycle.&nbsp;
And I'll explain to you what I mean by that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:21.040
Normally, you have a sort of relatively similar&nbsp;
electorate throughout three elections. So like if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:29.280
you look at, I don't know, 2000, 2002 and 2004,&nbsp;
as an example, the electorate George W Bush won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.960
the presidency in 2000. And then you had the&nbsp;
2002 midterms. And Bush won reelection in 2004.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:42.480
There was no major change to the electorate in the&nbsp;
year 2000, which then had to sort of sort itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.240
out in 2000, two. And in 2004, it was a very&nbsp;
similar electorate. Okay. That is not the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:56.880
right now. In 2016, you had all of these formerly&nbsp;
nonpolitical, nonvoting people who were enamored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.240
with Trump, a lot of low information voters, a lot&nbsp;
of extremists of different kinds, people who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.880
clueless. All these people were not participating&nbsp;
in politics before 2016. They appeared in 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:19.280
And then in 2018, some of them voted in the&nbsp;
midterms. And then in 2020, they were still around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.880
and trying to get Trump reelected and now Trump's&nbsp;
gone. And there's a real question as to do these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.880
Trumps vote in midterms where now Trump isn't even&nbsp;
a candidate and Trump is less visible and so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.760
other things are going on? Or do those people that&nbsp;
only entered the political space thanks to Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.840
presence, do they just kind of go back to whatever&nbsp;
they were doing before? I don't know what that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.480
but it didn't include voting. We don't know the&nbsp;
answer to that. But it's potentially something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:51.120
that will really change the makeup of the 2022&nbsp;
midterm electorate. So do I have a definitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:57.120
answer? I don't. But when we see Republicans were&nbsp;
winning every generic ballot three months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.920
Democrats are now winning every generic&nbsp;
ballot. That is a major change and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:08.320
potentially significant. So I'm not ready to&nbsp;
count out Democrats in 2022, but we must vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.440
We must get involved in campaigns, phone&nbsp;
banking, all of it. And we'll be doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.000
everything we can to cover these, to cover the&nbsp;
important races and the mid-terms at large.

